{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-bc1b2baf-b3dc-413a-a9f6-3214251014c5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a341dc20a104d534","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-t7iskpk6/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource context\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic converts reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary to the structured resource-context convention: message-embedded resource identity becomes structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) and `resourceKind` is added to all reconciliation error logs. The cloudmanager binary handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies (deployed on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave) with three reconciliation controllers — AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine — built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, which already provides `name`/`namespace` via the request logger at the reconcile entry point.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. **Audit**: enumerate all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three cloudmanager controllers, using grep/static analysis; produce a tracked call-site checklist recording the observed embedded-identity percentage.\n2. **Convert message-embedded identity**: for each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity and emit it as structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), leaving a clean message string. Where the shared reconciler's request logger already provides `name`/`namespace`, ensure error-path logs do not duplicate or contradict those fields.\n3. **Add `resourceKind`**: add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs — for these controllers the kind is not implied by the controller name in query terms, so it is a required field.\n4. **Cluster-scoped resources**: the cloudmanager engine resources are cluster-scoped; emit `name` and `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious namespace value.\n5. **Lint rule coverage**: verify the structured-log CI lint rule (flagging `log.Error` calls embedding name/namespace in message text or missing required fields) covers all cloudmanager-binary code paths, extending the rule's scope if needed. The rule's coverage of this binary's surface is delivered and verified here rather than in a separate enforcement epic.\n6. **Unit tests**: tests parsing test logger output to assert field presence and correctness for each of the three controllers.\n7. **Backwards-compatibility verification**: before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error, confirming structured fields appear and log consumers ingest both formats without configuration changes.\n\nField naming follows camelCase (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) per the Staff Engineer / SME input, matching controller-runtime's structured log key conventions.\n\nThis work is independently schedulable from the manager-binary conversion: the two binaries have disjoint controller sets. Whether it ships in the same release or a subsequent one is an open product decision; this epic makes no assumption about release timing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n- No cloudmanager reconciliation error log embeds resource name or namespace in its message string, measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis passing with zero violations over cloudmanager-binary code paths.\n- Cluster-scoped engine resources emit `name` and `resourceKind` with namespace omitted or empty, measured by a unit test confirming no spurious namespace value.\n- The audit deliverable exists as a tracked checklist covering all `log.Error` call sites in the cloudmanager binary, with the observed embedded-identity percentage recorded.\n- A before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error confirms structured fields appear and both formats are ingested by the production log consumer stack without configuration changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n| HLR | Priority | Coverage |\n|-----|----------|----------|\n| HLR-1 — reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace converted to structured KV pairs | P0 | Cloudmanager-binary portion: the three multi-cloud infrastructure controllers |\n| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | Full |\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":61630,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-865e0e4e9f1fc2aa","agent_id":"a7c2659a9f7c83256","session_id":"baaaf3ac-65f4-4aef-b7ec-53586eccdd51","uuid":"80b8d6ea-f35a-4c14-a60d-d6978ff3579b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:46:17.775Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
