{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-923a761e3be80507","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Decision\n13\t\n14\tFull decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). Although the strategy is S-sized, affects a single Jira component (rhods-operator), and is owned by a single team (ODH Operator team), the below-threshold escape condition for **multiple distinct work streams** applies:\n15\t\n16\t1. **Architecturally distinct sub-systems**: the rhods-operator repository ships two binaries with separate controller surfaces — the manager binary (DSC, 8 component controllers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers, 15 action files) and the cloudmanager binary (3 multi-cloud infrastructure controllers on the shared reconciler). These warrant separate PR review cycles, and the strategy itself raises whether cloudmanager lands in the same or a subsequent release.\n17\t2. **Code implementation + substantive content authoring**: beyond the code conversion, the strategy requires a new contribution guideline (P1 HLR) documenting the convention and publishing it for external operator adoption — new content authoring, not an incidental doc touch-up.\n18\t\n19\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n20\t\n21\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n22\t\n23\t| # | AI-generated text | SME correction | Effect on decomposition |\n24\t|---|------------------|----------------|------------------------|\n25\t| 1 | \"~16 component action files\" | Exactly 8 component action files; 15 total action files across four tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | Scope of the manager-binary epic uses the confirmed 15-file inventory |\n26\t| 2 | Architecture described as four controller tiers, omitting the module controller | Module controller tier exists with 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and 3 action files with error logs | Module controller work is in scope and covered by its own P0 HLR |\n27\t| 3 | Open question premised on \"~16 component action files, not all 8 components have action files\" | All 8 components have exactly one action file (8/8, confirmed by glob) | Question treated as answered; not carried as an unknown |\n28\t| 4 | Field naming unspecified in AI text | camelCase (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) matching controller-runtime convention | Convention fixed for all epics; no open design decision |\n29\t\n30\tSME-confirmed facts relied upon: three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), 5 controller tiers in the manager binary, 5 service controllers including Setup, cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler and already gets name/namespace, cluster-scoped resource AC, S effort estimate.\n31\t\n32\t## Component Mapping\n33\t\n34\tThe strategy's Affected Components table names **rhods-operator**, which is not a canonical RHAI Jira component. All epics use the canonical component **OpenShift AI Productization** (the RHOAI platform operator's productization component) — the closest canonical name for work on the rhods-operator codebase. The docs-authoring epic keeps the same component because the contribution guideline is repository-specific content owned by the same team, rather than platform-wide documentation. The Cluster Observability Operator team is a recipient of the published convention only (adoption at their discretion per platform policy overlay 0008) and owns no epic.\n35\t\n36\tAll three epics span the single strategy component rhods-operator (manager binary, cloudmanager binary, and repo contribution guideline respectively); each is assigned the single most-relevant canonical name above.\n37\t\n38\t## Epic List\n39\t\n40\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n41\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Structured log field convention guideline and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n45\t\n46\t## Dependency DAG\n47\t\n48\t```mermaid\n49\tgraph TD\n50\t    E001[\"E001: manager binary log conversion (P0)\"]\n51\t    E002[\"E002: cloudmanager binary log conversion (P0)\"]\n52\t    E003[\"E003: convention guideline + publication (P1, docs-authoring)\"]\n53\t    E001 --> E003\n54\t    E002 --> E003\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t## DAG Justification\n58\t\n59\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n60\t|------|------|-----------|\n61\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The contribution guideline documents the convention as implemented in the manager binary; docs describe what was built. |\n62\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The guideline also covers the cloudmanager controllers' convention; docs describe what was built. |\n63\t| *(non-edge)* E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries with disjoint controller sets. No shared artifacts are built; overlap is limited to coordination on the shared lint rule, which is handled by each epic owning its own surface's coverage (see below). Merge-conflict risk is a coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n64\t| *(non-edge)* no standalone lint epic | Step 5 shared-surface enforcement rule | The CI lint rule is an enforcement deliverable over the same surfaces being converted. Each binary's epic delivers and verifies the lint rule's coverage for its own code paths: the manager-binary epic authors the rule and verifies coverage over manager paths; the cloudmanager epic verifies coverage over cloudmanager paths. No standalone lint epic carves the logical change apart. |\n65\t| *(non-edge)* no Investigation epics | Rule 3 / Step 3 decision rule | Every open question fails the \"changes which epics exist or what they do\" test — see Open Question Disposition below. Each is an acceptance criterion or scheduling note on an existing epic. |\n66\t| *(non-edge)* no COO-team epic | Rule 21 / platform policy overlay 0008 | COO adoption (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is voluntary and externally controlled; the RHOAI-side deliverable is publishing the convention, which is scope of the docs epic. Filing tracking issues in COO projects is an acceptance criterion there, not a separate epic. |\n67\t\n68\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n69\t\n70\tHLRs are numbered in strategy order (HLR-1 … HLR-8).\n71\t\n72\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic(s) | Notes |\n73\t|-----|----------|------------------------|---------|-------|\n74\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Manager-binary call sites in E001; cloudmanager call sites in E002 (shared reconciler already provides name/namespace at entry, so E002's slice is converting embedded-identity messages plus resourceKind). |\n75\t| HLR-2 | P0 | DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller. |\n76\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager-binary service controllers. |\n77\t| HLR-4 | P0 | All 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | All action files are manager-binary paths. |\n78\t| HLR-5 | P0 | Cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary is a separate deliverable surface. |\n79\t| HLR-6 | P0 | Module controller error logs include the three fields across 8 module handlers | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Module controller tier (SME-corrected architecture). |\n80\t| HLR-7 | P1 | DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 **(bundled)** | Bundled, not split: the P1 work is the same `resourceKind` addition applied to the very same `log.Error` call sites in the same component controllers and DSC controller that the P0 conversion (HLR-1/HLR-4) already touches. It shares the identical code surface — splitting would force two passes over the same call sites and two PR review cycles over the same files, with no independent user value (the P1 field alone, without the P0 structured conversion, delivers nothing queryable). Not independently deferrable; recorded here per the bundling rule. |\n81\t| HLR-8 | P1 | Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Substantive content authoring — distinct work stream, separate docs-authoring epic. |\n82\t\n83\tAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n84\t\n85\t## Open Question Disposition (Step 3 scan)\n86\t\n87\tThe strategy carries four open questions plus risks and assumptions; each was evaluated against \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do\":\n88\t\n89\t| Unknown | Owner | Decision | Handling |\n90\t|---------|-------|----------|----------|\n91\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)? | PM / Observability | No structural change | Acceptance criterion on E003: the guideline records the decision on additional optional fields. The HLRs fix the required fields for implementation, so the answer cannot change what E001/E002 build — it only extends the guideline's content. |\n92\t| Which existing log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns that will change? | SRE / Support | No structural change | Acceptance criterion on E001 (and E002): identified and coordinated before merge as part of the backwards-compatibility verification. The identification work happens regardless of its outcome (fix-and-retry). |\n93\t| Should cloudmanager ship in the same release as manager, or follow subsequently? | PM / ODH Operator team | No structural change | Scheduling-only decision. E002 exists and does the same work either way; keeping it a separate parallel epic makes it independently schedulable, which satisfies both outcomes. Recorded in Health Warnings. |\n94\t| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity? | ODH Operator team | No structural change | The audit is the first work item of E001/E002 with a call-site checklist acceptance criterion. A different percentage changes the volume of the same conversion, not the epic structure (fix-and-retry). |\n95\t\n96\tThe strategy's Prerequisites & Process Gates (architecture review, build onboarding, license validation, etc.) are strategy-level signoff gates pending human review, not decomposition drivers: rhods-operator is an existing in-platform component with an architecture-context file, so no onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is generated.\n97\t\n98\t## Health Warnings\n99\t\n100\t1. **Epic size (E001), managed**: the manager-binary sweep is estimated at ~1–1.5 sprints of the strategy's S (1–2 sprint) total — at or slightly above the ~2-week epic guideline. It is kept intact as **one logical change over an enumerated surface**: a single mechanical conversion (message-embedded identity → structured KV, plus resourceKind) applied uniformly to every controller and action file in the manager binary, which the strategy's own work breakdown presents as one work item. Splitting it (e.g. by controller tier) would fragment the lint-rule ownership and force repeated passes over shared review context. **Conditional re-split trigger**: if the validated audit of `log.Error` call sites materially exceeds the strategy's 75% embedded-identity estimate (i.e. the audited checklist shows substantially more conversion sites than planned), or capacity constraints require independently schedulable units, re-split E001 along controller-tier seams (DSCI key migration; service + module + DSC tiers; component-controller tier).\n101\t2. **Unvalidated audit premise**: the \"75% of log calls embed resource identity\" figure is an SME estimate requiring codebase confirmation. It gates effort, not structure (see Open Question Disposition).\n102\t3. **Log consumer regression risk**: text-based alert rules and log ingestion (Loki, CloudWatch) may break when message strings change. Mitigation is an acceptance criterion (before/after comparison with the production log consumer stack on a dev cluster, SRE/Support coordination) on the implementation epics, per the strategy's NFR.\n103\t4. **External adoption timing**: COO team adoption of the published convention is voluntary and outside RHOAI control (platform policy overlay 0008). The docs epic's deliverable is publication plus tracking issues; adoption is best-effort and explicitly not an acceptance criterion.\n104\t5. **Cloudmanager release timing** (open PM question): E002 may be deferred to a subsequent release without affecting E001 or E003's guideline content — E003 depends on E002 completing, so a deferral slides the guideline's completeness; the guideline could alternatively document the manager implementation first. Flagged for PM decision during planning.\n105\t6. **Pending strategy signoff**: the strategy's Acceptance Criteria are marked \"requires PM/Engineering validation\" and all Prerequisites & Process Gates are pending human review — these are strategy-level gates that precede epic execution, not epic scope.\n106\t7. **Acceptance-criteria provenance**: the strategy's AC section embeds \"measured by\" clauses (CI lint rule, unit tests) that are carried into the epic acceptance criteria verbatim in intent; the lint rule is co-delivered by the implementation epics per the shared-surface division rule.\n107\t\n108\t## Tiered Delivery\n109\t\n110\tNo dependency-gated tiering is required: the only external dependency (COO team adoption) gates nothing on the RHOAI side — the deliverable is publication, and adoption is voluntary per platform policy, so there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split around it. The manager and cloudmanager conversions are parallel, independently shippable units; the open PM question on cloudmanager release timing is a scheduling choice (cloudmanager may follow in a subsequent release), not a dependency tier, and is captured in Health Warnings.\n111\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4c24c37a819e3d5","agent_id":"a447e31c7c13a529d","session_id":"baaaf3ac-65f4-4aef-b7ec-53586eccdd51","uuid":"30c6283a-8679-4333-b2e8-3af787280a0e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:48:30.869Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-923a761e3be80507","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
