{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5f33cd34ac1de28","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 0\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 6\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic converts reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary to the structured resource-context convention: message-embedded resource identity becomes structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) and `resourceKind` is added to all reconciliation error logs. The cloudmanager binary handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies (deployed on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave) with three reconciliation controllers — AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine — built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, which already provides `name`/`namespace` via the request logger at the reconcile entry point.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t1. **Audit**: enumerate all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three cloudmanager controllers, using grep/static analysis; produce a tracked call-site checklist recording the observed embedded-identity percentage.\n39\t2. **Convert message-embedded identity**: for each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity and emit it as structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), leaving a clean message string. Where the shared reconciler's request logger already provides `name`/`namespace`, ensure error-path logs do not duplicate or contradict those fields.\n40\t3. **Add `resourceKind`**: add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs — for these controllers the kind is not implied by the controller name in query terms, so it is a required field.\n41\t4. **Cluster-scoped resources**: the cloudmanager engine resources are cluster-scoped; emit `name` and `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious namespace value.\n42\t5. **Lint rule coverage**: verify the structured-log CI lint rule (flagging `log.Error` calls embedding name/namespace in message text or missing required fields) covers all cloudmanager-binary code paths, extending the rule's scope if needed. The rule's coverage of this binary's surface is delivered and verified here rather than in a separate enforcement epic.\n43\t6. **Unit tests**: tests parsing test logger output to assert field presence and correctness for each of the three controllers.\n44\t7. **Backwards-compatibility verification**: before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error, confirming structured fields appear and log consumers ingest both formats without configuration changes.\n45\t\n46\tField naming follows camelCase (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) per the Staff Engineer / SME input, matching controller-runtime's structured log key conventions.\n47\t\n48\tThis work is independently schedulable from the manager-binary conversion: the two binaries have disjoint controller sets. Whether it ships in the same release or a subsequent one is an open product decision; this epic makes no assumption about release timing.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n53\t- No cloudmanager reconciliation error log embeds resource name or namespace in its message string, measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis passing with zero violations over cloudmanager-binary code paths.\n54\t- Cluster-scoped engine resources emit `name` and `resourceKind` with namespace omitted or empty, measured by a unit test confirming no spurious namespace value.\n55\t- The audit deliverable exists as a tracked checklist covering all `log.Error` call sites in the cloudmanager binary, with the observed embedded-identity percentage recorded.\n56\t- A before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error confirms structured fields appear and both formats are ingested by the production log consumer stack without configuration changes.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t| HLR | Priority | Coverage |\n61\t|-----|----------|----------|\n62\t| HLR-1 — reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace converted to structured KV pairs | P0 | Cloudmanager-binary portion: the three multi-cloud infrastructure controllers |\n63\t| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | Full |\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-adedd3562747cce6","agent_id":"a986b9c2efa209945","session_id":"baaaf3ac-65f4-4aef-b7ec-53586eccdd51","uuid":"7b1c0c59-e782-4139-adb7-62ef82c60bba","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:51:43.277Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5f33cd34ac1de28","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
